;;; **The `MAX_ALT_JUSTIFICATIONS = 32` cap could enlarge the reported unsat
;;; core — and hand it back the order-dependence the AND/OR search removed.**
;;; Regression `alt-cap-core`.
;;;
;;; This file and [`alt-cap-core-reordered.ein`](alt-cap-core-reordered.ein)
;;; differ in the **written order** of one rule, `mk-w3` (§ The written
;;; order, below). Same facts, same rules, same derivations, same verdict.
;;; `ein solve` on each, by default and with
;;; `(config :record-alternative-justifications false)`:
;;;
;;;                                default          the flag off
;;;   alt-cap-core.ein             (w1 X) (w2 X)    (p1 Y01) (p2 Y01) (p3 Y01)
;;;   alt-cap-core-reordered.ein   (w1 X) (w2 X)    (w1 X) (w2 X)
;;;
;;; ── The shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(false)` is derivable 33 ways through `narrow` — one premise each, and
;;; that premise `(q Y..)` is itself three givens deep — and **once** through
;;; `wide`, two premises straight from givens. The smallest frontier over the
;;; derivations is therefore `{(w1 X), (w2 X)}`, size 2.
;;;
;;; `Kb::record_justification` keeps at most 32 *alternatives* per fact, sorted
;;; by **premise count**, and refuses from that list an arrival no shorter than
;;; the longest kept. Fire `narrow` first and the list fills with 32
;;; one-premise entries; `wide` then arrives with two, `2 >= 1`, and is
;;; refused from the list. A store that kept only the list would never hold
;;; the derivation with the smaller frontier, so `explain.rs` could not choose
;;; it and would report the three-fact chain instead: the defect this row
;;; records.
;;;
;;; Retention is by premise count, which is **local**. The search minimises
;;; frontier size, which is **transitive**. A one-premise step whose premise
;;; unfolds into a deep chain wins the first metric and loses the second, and
;;; that gap is the whole of it.
;;;
;;; ── Why the order-dependence is the sharper half ───────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `explain.rs`'s ATMS search exists *because* walking one justification per
;;; fact made the core depend on the order rules fired. A list that refuses
;;; re-introduces exactly that, one level up: the search is order-independent
;;; over what the store **kept**, and what the store keeps depends on the order
;;; the rules fired. A core is an explanation, and may vary with the order
;;; rules are written in (Q-M1f.6); this pair shows by how much.
;;;
;;; ── What it reports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A full list keeps, **beside** its 32, the arrival with the smallest closure
;;; (a derivation's frontier estimate: 1 a given, the sum of its premises'
;;; otherwise), so a core is the smallest over every derivation the program
;;; records (Q-M1e2.11). `wide`'s closure is 2 against `narrow`'s 3, so its
;;; derivation is kept beside the full list and the search is offered it:
;;; both files report `(w1 X) (w2 X)`, the core an uncapped store reports,
;;; and `refuted by` names `wide`, the rule of the core's derivation, in both.
;;; With `:record-alternative-justifications false` no alternative is
;;; recorded, a core is the smallest over each fact's first derivation, and
;;; this file reports the 3-fact core again, refuted by `narrow`: the flag may
;;; change the core, and its rule with it, and nothing else
;;; (`docs/ein/user/04-reference/06-config.md` § 2.5). And a saving *between* two
;;; facts, behind a full list, is still refused —
;;; [`wrong-answers/alt-cap-shared.ein`](wrong-answers/alt-cap-shared.ein),
;;; a known gap.
;;;
;;; This file is the shape that makes the promise's limit visible, not a
;;; report that a shipped puzzle is wrong.
;;;
;;; ── The written order ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No rule has a priority: the agenda and the boundary fire first what leads
;;; to `(false)`, then the rest, FIFO in the written and arrival order within
;;; each half (Q-M1f.313). `narrow` and `wide` both conclude `(false)`, so
;;; each fires as soon as its premises arrive. `wide` reads `(w3 ?x)`, which
;;; `mk-w3` derives from `(w1 ?x)`, so `(w3 X)`'s closure is `(w1 X)` and the
;;; core's facts are the givens'. Here `mk-w3` is written after `mk-q`: the 33
;;; `(q …)` and their `narrow` candidates arrive before `(w3 X)` exists, so
;;; `narrow` fills the list and `wide` arrives at a full one. The twin writes
;;; `mk-w3` first, so `wide` fires before anything derives a `(q …)`.
;;;
;;; ── Re-taking the table ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   ein solve examples/ein-bugs/alt-cap-core.ein
;;;   ein solve examples/ein-bugs/alt-cap-core-reordered.ein
;;;
;;; print the default column on their `unsat core` and `refuted by` rows; a
;;; copy of each with the `(config …)` line above at its head prints the
;;; other. Nothing else moves between the four runs: root's own fixpoint
;;; derives `(false)`, so every node is dead and R is empty, and the
;;; `(solve :expect (false))` below holds on all four.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p1 T :why "{?1} has p1")
(relation p2 T :why "{?1} has p2")
(relation p3 T :why "{?1} has p3")
(relation q  T :why "{?1} is a witness")
(relation w1 T :why "{?1} has w1")
(relation w2 T :why "{?1} has w2")
(relation w3 T :why "{?1} has w3")

;; 33 witnesses, each three givens deep.
(p1 Y01) (p2 Y01) (p3 Y01) (p1 Y02) (p2 Y02) (p3 Y02) (p1 Y03) (p2 Y03) (p3 Y03)
(p1 Y04) (p2 Y04) (p3 Y04) (p1 Y05) (p2 Y05) (p3 Y05) (p1 Y06) (p2 Y06) (p3 Y06)
(p1 Y07) (p2 Y07) (p3 Y07) (p1 Y08) (p2 Y08) (p3 Y08) (p1 Y09) (p2 Y09) (p3 Y09)
(p1 Y10) (p2 Y10) (p3 Y10) (p1 Y11) (p2 Y11) (p3 Y11) (p1 Y12) (p2 Y12) (p3 Y12)
(p1 Y13) (p2 Y13) (p3 Y13) (p1 Y14) (p2 Y14) (p3 Y14) (p1 Y15) (p2 Y15) (p3 Y15)
(p1 Y16) (p2 Y16) (p3 Y16) (p1 Y17) (p2 Y17) (p3 Y17) (p1 Y18) (p2 Y18) (p3 Y18)
(p1 Y19) (p2 Y19) (p3 Y19) (p1 Y20) (p2 Y20) (p3 Y20) (p1 Y21) (p2 Y21) (p3 Y21)
(p1 Y22) (p2 Y22) (p3 Y22) (p1 Y23) (p2 Y23) (p3 Y23) (p1 Y24) (p2 Y24) (p3 Y24)
(p1 Y25) (p2 Y25) (p3 Y25) (p1 Y26) (p2 Y26) (p3 Y26) (p1 Y27) (p2 Y27) (p3 Y27)
(p1 Y28) (p2 Y28) (p3 Y28) (p1 Y29) (p2 Y29) (p3 Y29) (p1 Y30) (p2 Y30) (p3 Y30)
(p1 Y31) (p2 Y31) (p3 Y31) (p1 Y32) (p2 Y32) (p3 Y32) (p1 Y33) (p2 Y33) (p3 Y33)

;; …and one two-given pair, which is the smaller explanation.
(w1 X) (w2 X)

(rule mk-q ()
  :match  (and (p1 ?y) (p2 ?y) (p3 ?y))
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why    "{?y} has all three")

;; `(w1 X)` read again as `(w3 X)`: written after `mk-q`, so it fires after
;; every `(q …)` is derived and `wide` after every `narrow`.
(rule mk-w3 ()
  :match  (w1 ?x)
  :assert (w3 ?x)
  :why    "{?x} has w1")

;; One premise, and that premise is three givens deep. Fires 33 times, which
;; is one primary plus a full list of 32 alternatives.
(rule narrow ()
  :match  (q ?y)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?y} is a witness")

;; Two premises, `(w3 X)` from the given `(w1 X)` and the given `(w2 X)`:
;; the smallest frontier there is.
(rule wide ()
  :match  (and (w3 ?x) (w2 ?x))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has both")

(solve :expect (false))

(query :goal (q ?y))
